17:02:13 | INFO | 👤 Proxy: Wait, but if a studio pays for the rights when they make the movie, why wouldn't that just cover it forever no matter what new format comes out?
17:02:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Because paying for “the rights” usually does **not** mean buying the song outright. It means buying a **license**: permission to use it under specified conditions. A typical music license might say, in effect: > You may use this recording in this film, in theaters, in the United States, for seven years. Or it may cover television and home video, but not streaming, international distribution, advertising, game adaptations, or future formats. The songwriter/publisher and record label still own their rights. They can charge separately for broader or later uses. Why not simply demand “forever, in every format”? - **It costs more.** A worldwide, perpetual, all-media license can be vastly more expensive than a limited theatrical or TV license. - **The rights holder may refuse.** Artists, labels, and publishers may not want to give up control over unknown future uses, or may want the chance to renegotiate later. - **Future media may be hard to define.** In older contracts, nobody knew about streaming, digital downloads, VR, etc. Courts and contracts do not always treat a phrase like “all media” as automatically covering every unforeseen technology. - **Productions operate under budgets and deadlines.** A studio may choose a cheaper limited license because it is enough to release the movie at the time. - **There can be multiple rightsholders.** The song publisher, record label, performers, estates, and foreign rights organizations may all have separate interests. One missing approval can prevent continued use. Modern studios often *do* try to secure licenses described as “worldwide, in perpetuity, in all media now known or hereafter devised.” If they got that language and all required rights, the music generally can stay. But especially in older films and TV, the initial license was often narrower—so when the movie later moves to DVD, streaming, or a new country, the distributor must either pay again or replace the song.
