17:02:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is normal too. Many movies—especially major studio releases—**did get broad, long-term licenses**, so the original soundtrack remains intact across VHS, DVD, Blu-ray, television, and streaming. A few things make this seem more contradictory than it is: - **Not every license expires.** Some were negotiated as perpetual, worldwide, all-media (or broad enough to cover later formats). - **Movies were often cleared more comprehensively than television.** A feature film expected to have a long commercial life could justify paying for broad rights. Older TV shows, especially those made for broadcast, often licensed music only for a certain number of broadcasts or years. - **Home video was already foreseeable in the 1980s.** VHS, cable, and later DVD rights were often included or renegotiated before release. A film from 1985 may have had a deal covering “home video” even if it did not specifically say “DVD.” - **Streaming rights may have been added later without changing the music.** The studio could renegotiate with the rightsholders, or the existing contract may have been broad enough to include it. - **Renewal does not necessarily mean replacement.** If a license term ends, the distributor can simply pay to renew it. You only notice the cases where they decide renewal is too costly or impossible. - **The problem is uneven.** A few famous replacements get a lot of attention, but the far more common outcome is that nobody notices anything because the original music remains. Also, music changes are particularly common in certain categories: TV series with lots of pop songs, low-budget movies, reality shows, music-heavy scenes, international editions, and streaming versions of older programs. A prominent 1980s theatrical film with a commercially important soundtrack was more likely to have its music cleared carefully—and may even have had soundtrack-album and promotional arrangements that encouraged keeping the songs in place.
