17:02:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: For **feature films**, your experience is closer to the general rule: the music is usually cleared broadly enough that it stays with the movie for its commercial life. So the better answer is not “old movie music licenses routinely expire and get replaced.” They generally do **not**. A studio making a major theatrical film typically wants the song to remain part of the film forever, because replacing it later can damage the work and create a mess across all existing versions. But that permanence is a result of the **contract they negotiated**, not an automatic legal rule. The original deal may effectively be: - use this song in this specific motion picture, - worldwide, - in all media, - in perpetuity. If that is what was licensed—both the song itself and the particular recording—then VHS, DVD, Blu-ray, streaming, and later formats can generally use it without another clearance. Your observation that you have never noticed a replacement is therefore good evidence that: 1. broad/perpetual licenses are very common for the movies you watch; 2. studios often renew or settle rights issues quietly when needed; and 3. actual soundtrack replacement in theatrical movies is uncommon enough that it stands out when it happens. The “songs get swapped because rights expired” story is much more associated with **older television**, especially shows with lots of licensed pop music. TV episodes were often cleared only for broadcast runs or a limited number of airings, because producers did not originally expect lucrative home-video and streaming afterlives. That is why people notice altered music much more in old TV releases than in movies. So: no, rights do not automatically lock in forever merely because a film was made—but, in practice, for most studio movies, the relevant rights were secured broadly enough that the original songs stay put.
